## 2. `describeCheckout` — Option Bag Junk Drawer (agent_risk 0.38, lines 10–19)

The finding flags "implicit shape / hard to know which fields are required." Worth being precise: the shape here *is* declared (`CheckoutOptions`, all six required). The real hazard is the composition — **five of the six fields are bare `string`**, so they're positionally interchangeable, and the function joins them in a load-bearing order:

```ts
return [options.plan, options.role, options.region, options.currency, options.status, String(options.retry)].join(":");
```

Swap `region` and `currency` at a call site and it type-checks and produces a wrong key.

**Refactor — narrow the types and group by ownership:**

```ts
import { PLANS, type Plan } from "./billing";        // see crime_00004: "enterprise" scattered across 4 files
type Role = "owner" | "admin" | "member";
type CheckoutStatus = "pending" | "paid" | "failed";

interface CheckoutSubject { plan: Plan; role: Role }
interface CheckoutLocale  { region: string; currency: string }
interface CheckoutAttempt { status: CheckoutStatus; retry: boolean }

export function describeCheckout(
  subject: CheckoutSubject,
  locale: CheckoutLocale,
  attempt: CheckoutAttempt,
): string {
  return [subject.plan, subject.role, locale.region, locale.currency, attempt.status, String(attempt.retry)].join(":");
}
```

Three narrow records instead of one flat bag: each argument names what it's for, and the literal unions make `plan`/`role`/`status` non-substitutable. Pulling `Plan` from a shared definition also closes the loop on the `"enterprise"` scatter finding (`crime_00004`), which currently duplicates that policy across four files.

**What an agent gets wrong today:** asked to "include the coupon code in the checkout description", an agent adds a seventh `string` to `CheckoutOptions` and drops it into the array — plausibly *in the middle*, next to `currency`, because that reads well. Every previously-generated string changes. And the agent has no way to know whether this value is a display label (safe to reorder) or a cache/idempotency key (reordering is a production incident), because nothing in the file says. Name the return type — `type CheckoutKey = string` with a one-line comment on stability — or move the ordering into an explicit named segment list.

---

## 3. `canRunBilling` — Negative Flag Maze (agent_risk 0.36, line 35)

```ts
export function canRunBilling(disableBilling: boolean, skipRetry: boolean) {
  if (!disableBilling && !skipRetry) return true;
  return false;
}
```

Two negatives resolved into a positive, plus two same-typed positional booleans — call sites can transpose the arguments with no type error, and the `if (cond) return true; return false` shape is just the expression with extra places to insert a mistake.

**Refactor — negate once, at the config boundary:**

```ts
export interface BillingSwitches {
  billingEnabled: boolean;
  retryEnabled: boolean;
}

export function canRunBilling({ billingEnabled, retryEnabled }: BillingSwitches): boolean {
  return billingEnabled && retryEnabled;
}

// Only place a negative name survives — adapt persisted/legacy config here.
export function billingSwitchesFrom(config: { disableBilling: boolean; skipRetry: boolean }): BillingSwitches {
  return { billingEnabled: !config.disableBilling, retryEnabled: !config.skipRetry };
}
```

The named-object parameter also removes the argument-transposition risk entirely.

**What an agent gets wrong today:** asked to "also block billing for delinquent accounts", the agent has to extend a double negative. The correct edit is `&& !isDelinquent`, but under a De Morgan slip the natural-sounding `!(disableBilling && skipRetry && isDelinquent)` inverts the whole predicate — billing runs when it's disabled. With `return billingEnabled && retryEnabled && !isDelinquent`, the new term reads the same way as the existing ones and there's nothing to invert.

Separately: `skipRetry` gating *whether billing runs at all* conflates a retry policy with a kill switch. Worth confirming that's intended before extending it — if it isn't, that's a behaviour bug the flag naming is hiding.

